
Here's a RAG architecture that actually scales.

1. First, test with real-world chaos.Mix 95% garbage data with your real documents.

If your pipeline can still find the right answers in all that noise, you know it's solid.

2. Process your documents in parallel, not one by one. Tools like Ray let you parse thousands of files 
across multiple machines at the same time.

3. Don't just use Vector Search alone. Combine it with a knowledge graph. Vector gives you semantic meaning. The graph 
gives you relationships between entities. Run both at the same time.

4. Never put your LLM inside your API server. Host your model separately using something like the LLM and Ray serve. So 
they scale based on GPU demand without killing your web server.

5. Use multiple databases each for what it's best at. One for chat history, one for caching, one for vectors, 
one for entity relationships. One database can't do everything well.

6. Add semantic caching. If two users ask the same question in different words, recognize it's the same intent 
and serve a cached answer. Zero GPU cost.

7. Make your agent smart, not linear. Use something like LLM graph to build a state machine where the agent can plan, 
decide if it needs to search, run code, or answer directly. It can loop and correct itself instead of just going retrieve, 
then answer.

8. If your agent can execute code, sandbox it. No network access, limited memory, hard time out.

9. One bad prompt shouldn't take down your system.

10. Define your entire cloud infrastructure as code using tools like Terraform.

11. Don't click buttons in a console. And use something like carpenter to scale to zero.

12. When nobody's using it, kill the GPU machines automatically. When traffic comes back, spin them up in seconds. 
Only pay for what you use.

13. The secret is separation. Your models, your API, your databases, your tools, all independent, all scaling on their own.

That's production grade rag.

